The relationship ends only when you are no longer paid and no longer have responsibilities — a resignation letter does not end it.
BEFORE the relationship ends, with notice given, she MAY name her new employer, but must NOT (without the current employer's permission):
• give her new contact information;
• describe the new firm's services or otherwise implicitly/explicitly promote it;
• contact existing or prospective clients about a change in employment status before notice is given at all;
• disparage the current employer (a violation even if the client would be better off elsewhere).
AFTER departure she may contact former clients using PUBLIC information — provided the contact details do not come from firm records or her work there, and no nonsolicitation/non-compete agreement applies.
• Whistle-blower route: if practices are egregious enough to harm clients, resign first, then notify former clients or appropriate parties.
• Not a violation: updating a LinkedIn profile after departure, even though the platform auto-notifies her whole network.

Required, once a violation is discovered:
• respond promptly;
• initiate/ensure a thorough investigation to determine the scope of the wrongdoing;
• increase supervision or place appropriate limitations on the alleged offender PENDING the outcome of the investigation;
• review procedures for changes needed to prevent recurrence.
Explicitly NOT sufficient:
• relying on the employee's own account of how far it went, or assurances it will not happen again;
• reporting to compliance and management and warning the employee to stop, then resuming normal operations.
• Related trap: dismissing a subordinate's concern ("don't ask questions, the fund is too successful") violates IV(C) for both the supervisor and the compliance officer — they must review concerns brought to them.
